OK.

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.630
CYRIL TOVENA: So some very
simple dedicated game servers,

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:49.560
they just forward back
the actual information

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.290
from one player to
the other players.

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:51.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:53.220
CYRIL TOVENA: It could
be a very easy example.

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:53.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:54.660
MARK MANDEL: But
other game servers

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:55.890
will probably do,
like, full physics

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:56.780
simulation and everything--

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:57.150
CYRIL TOVENA: Yeah, exactly.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:58.680
MARK MANDEL: --that's actually
happening inside the game

00:07:58.680 --> 00:07:59.890
and what it's doing.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:01.050
So yeah, why Agones?

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.420
So hosting and scaling
dedicated game servers

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:06.760
is a little different from
other types of applications.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.480
So if you look at something like
a traditional web application--

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:10.220
it's stateless.

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:11.760
You could spin up
100,000 of them.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:13.260
And if you kill
50,000 at one point,

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:15.480
as long as there's enough to
handle the load, nobody cares.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:16.950
If you run a
database, the database

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:19.360
runs for probably
months or years on end.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:21.344
So that's data you want
to persist to a disk.

00:08:21.344 --> 00:08:23.010
Dedicated game servers,
I feel like this

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:24.090
is the thing that
sticks with me,

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.590
is like they sit
in this weird space

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.590
where before players
connect to them,

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:31.290
you could run 100,000 of
them, kill 50,000 of them,

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:32.440
and no one would care.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.110
But as soon as players
connect to those

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:35.370
dedicated game
servers-- and they're

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:36.150
doing direct connections.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:37.669
They're not going
through a load balancer.

00:08:37.669 --> 00:08:40.127
As soon as you have a direct
connection and players playing

00:08:40.127 --> 00:08:43.470
on them, you cannot kill those,
because players get really mad.

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:44.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Kind
of makes sense, yeah.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.060
CYRIL TOVENA: And it's very
difficult to connect them back

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:46.810
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:48.370
CYRIL TOVENA: --to
another session.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:51.120
MARK MANDEL: So Agones exists to
handle those sort of problems.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.120
So being able to create
these game servers either

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.550
on demand or potentially
[? warm ?] versions of them

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:59.490
beforehand, and making
sure to control and manage

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.159
the lifecycle of
those game servers

00:09:01.159 --> 00:09:03.450
so that once players are
connected to them, even if you

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:04.824
want to update
those game servers

00:09:04.824 --> 00:09:06.460
or you want to scale
down or scale up,

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.543
you're not going to interrupt
those game sessions,

00:09:08.543 --> 00:09:09.120
essentially.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.036
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:10.036 --> 00:09:13.380
So you're using the term
"dedicated game server."

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.210
What is the other
alternative, when

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:16.870
it's not dedicated and server?

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:19.107
How do you do this?

00:09:19.107 --> 00:09:20.940
CYRIL TOVENA: It's
actually peer connection.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:24.710
So you connect players
altogether without a server.

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:26.730
So they directly
connect with each other.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:27.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, I see.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:27.850
CYRIL TOVENA: That's
another option.

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:27.970
Yeah.

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:28.385
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:28.385 --> 00:09:29.770
MELANIE WARWICK: And
who is using this?

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:31.210
Who's taking advantage of this?

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:32.430
MARK MANDEL: Of
dedicated game servers?

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:33.170
MELANIE WARWICK:
Yes, of using Agones?

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.461
MARK MANDEL: So Agones right
now is still fairly early.

00:09:35.461 --> 00:09:36.000
It's alpha.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.855
So literally, as of right now,
I am doing a 0.3.0 release.

00:09:40.855 --> 00:09:41.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yay!

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:43.170
MARK MANDEL: So we've got some
people who are playing with it

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:45.371
and some people who are
kind of working against it,

00:09:45.371 --> 00:09:47.870
nothing that we can specifically
mention right now, I think,

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:48.670
is probably pretty accurate.

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:49.110
CYRIL TOVENA: Yep.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:50.000
MELANIE WARWICK:
Working against it?

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:50.790
MARK MANDEL: Working
against it, you

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:52.331
know, trying it out
for a few things.

00:09:52.331 --> 00:09:53.370
I think that works.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:54.370
So we're getting
a lot of feedback,

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:55.570
which is really, really good.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:56.700
We've still got some
features that we

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:58.650
want to have done on
the roadmap and will be

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.030
needed for a full 1.0 release.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:01.680
But we're moving
forward pretty quick.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.596
MELANIE WARWICK: And how
does the relationship

00:10:03.596 --> 00:10:04.440
work with Ubisoft?

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.481
MARK MANDEL: They tell me
what to do and I do it.

00:10:06.481 --> 00:10:07.819
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:07.819 --> 00:10:09.069
CYRIL TOVENA: It's close, huh?

00:10:09.069 --> 00:10:09.685
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:09.685 --> 00:10:10.560
MARK MANDEL: Kind of.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.300
CYRIL TOVENA:
Yeah, well, for me,

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:15.630
I think the relationship is
that Google Cloud is very

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:17.270
good at scaling containers.

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:18.910
They've been doing
that for a while,

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:20.035
especially with Kubernetes.

00:10:20.035 --> 00:10:21.820
You're the cradle of
Kubernetes, so that's

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:24.360
why we are collaborating
together on this project.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:28.230
And on all sides, I think since
we have a lot of online games,

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:31.020
we have the expertise of
running those online games.

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:33.450
And we can help
build that project,

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.010
and we know actually
what it takes

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:36.597
to run dedicated game servers.

00:10:36.597 --> 00:10:39.180
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I just want
to say it's been a real delight

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:40.560
and a pleasure working
with you on this project.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:41.310
CYRIL TOVENA: Yes.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:41.810
Same for me.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:42.976
MELANIE WARWICK: Well, good.

00:10:42.976 --> 00:10:44.490
Well, where did
the name come from?

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:45.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:47.030
CYRIL TOVENA: And how
do you pronounce it?

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:47.740
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:49.840
So actually, I had this
conversation a little

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:51.090
while ago with a person I met.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.267
Apparently I should
actually be saying "Ahones."

00:10:53.267 --> 00:10:54.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: "Ahones"?

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:55.650
MARK MANDEL: I could
be-- no, so I'm

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:56.490
going to say this
with a grain of salt.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:58.114
I was told by someone
who speaks Greek.

00:10:58.114 --> 00:11:00.950
So apparently Agones with
a hard G means elbows.

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:04.500
And "Ahones" comes from
a public competition.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.130
It was like a precursor
to the Olympic games.

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:09.584
So normally at a public
gathering, you would have--

00:11:09.584 --> 00:11:11.250
I love using the term
feats of strength.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:12.970
But you'd have sporting
games and that kind of stuff.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:14.846
So people would gather
for big public events,

00:11:14.846 --> 00:11:16.178
and there would be competitions.

00:11:16.178 --> 00:11:17.640
And I felt like
that would be kind

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:20.580
of appropriate for basically a
platform for multiplayer games.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.176
Also, I got it
through legal review.

00:11:22.176 --> 00:11:22.852
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:22.852 --> 00:11:24.060
So, you know, there was that.

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.809
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's interesting.

00:11:25.809 --> 00:11:27.880
So you're supposed to
pronounce it "Ahones"

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:29.010
or something like that?

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:30.256
How do you pronounce it?

00:11:30.256 --> 00:11:32.130
MARK MANDEL: I have a
tendency to say Agones,

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:33.600
which I think is probably
more amusing, because it

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:34.140
means elbows.

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:35.056
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sure.

00:11:35.056 --> 00:11:36.420
MARK MANDEL: But I don't mind.

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.910
As long as you try the
platform and see if it works.

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:39.600
MELANIE WARWICK:
As well as maybe

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:40.480
contribute to the platform?

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:41.820
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
contributing's great too.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.278
MELANIE WARWICK: What are the
unique challenges for running

00:11:44.278 --> 00:11:45.330
dedicated game servers?

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.913
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You mentioned
before the fact that you cannot

00:11:47.913 --> 00:11:50.190
just like downscale easily.

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:51.290
What other things there?

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:52.998
MARK MANDEL: OK, so
yeah, we talked a bit

00:11:52.998 --> 00:11:55.667
before about how scaling down,
you can't just kill players.

00:11:55.667 --> 00:11:57.250
They tend to get mad
when you do that.

00:11:57.250 --> 00:11:58.980
So that's definitely
a unique challenge.

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.834
I think dedicated game
servers definitely also have--

00:12:01.834 --> 00:12:03.250
well, outside of
the Agones space,

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:05.750
they have challenges
around CPU performance.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.320
They're very CPU bound.

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:09.390
So you're doing a lot
of physics simulations

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:11.860
and sending a lot
of network traffic.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:13.860
Network latency, we were
talking about that too,

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:14.980
is definitely a thing as well.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:16.480
CYRIL TOVENA:
Versioning [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:18.660
is very complicated with
the client and the server.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:20.127
It's very complicated to handle.

00:12:20.127 --> 00:12:21.960
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so
there's communication,

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:23.126
things you need to consider.

00:12:23.126 --> 00:12:25.280
So much like, say, for
example, if you were doing,

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:27.480
say, like a gRPC or
a REST-based service,

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:29.650
as you change as one
side goes to another,

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:31.733
you need to change both
the client and the server.

00:12:31.733 --> 00:12:33.837
The same thing exists with
dedicated game servers.

00:12:33.837 --> 00:12:34.920
CYRIL TOVENA: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.760
It needs to be available
pretty quickly, also.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:38.510
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:40.740
Players tend to get mad if
they can't get on games really

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:41.100
quickly.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:41.380
CYRIL TOVENA: They
don't like the wait.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:42.690
MARK MANDEL: They really
don't like to wait.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:43.550
They get really, really mad.

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:44.490
CYRIL TOVENA: Or they
play another game

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:45.540
if they have to wait.

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:46.960
MELANIE WARWICK: That
could be a problem.

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:49.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How does
Kubernetes help with that?

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:51.400
What are the
components or features

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:55.200
of Kubernetes that make it
so Agones is a good match?

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.450
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, this is
where I get really excited.

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:58.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:01.380
Kubernetes gives
a lot of the base

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:04.740
building blocks for
basically running processes

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.647
on big clusters of machines,
I think at a base level.

00:13:07.647 --> 00:13:08.730
I think that's what it is.

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:12.455
And it has a declarative and API
driven approach to doing that.

00:13:12.455 --> 00:13:13.830
And the other
thing that it gives

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:16.470
that I think is really exciting,
what makes Agones possible,

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:18.445
is it gives native
extensions internally.

00:13:18.445 --> 00:13:20.070
So if you are familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.170
you have pods and
deployments and services,

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:23.610
like those kind of nouns.

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:26.640
And the phrase I like to use
is you can add your own nouns.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.997
So Agones, for example, where
like here's a game server noun.

00:13:29.997 --> 00:13:31.830
And we're going to tell
it how to extend it.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:34.150
It's called a custom resource
definition and a controller.

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:36.030
But at the end of the day,
we're like, you install Agones.

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:37.860
And now suddenly
Kubernetes is like, oh,

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.360
I know how to
manage game servers.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:39.941
That's cool.

00:13:39.941 --> 00:13:41.940
Oh, I know how to manage
fleets of game servers,

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:43.500
because now I know
what a fleet is.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:45.150
And I know how to manage
those in a declarative way

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:46.650
in exactly the same
way as you would

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:48.630
do with a YAML file
for a deployment.

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:51.057
You can do, say, a YAML file
for a fleet or a game server.

00:13:51.057 --> 00:13:52.890
By the same token, you
can do the same thing

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:54.240
like through the Kubernetes API.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:56.573
It extends both the YAML
definitions that you can apply,

00:13:56.573 --> 00:13:57.960
as well as the Kubernetes API.

00:13:57.960 --> 00:13:59.730
So Kubernetes knows
what game servers

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:02.580
are and can manage life
cycles and that kind of stuff.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:02.690
CYRIL TOVENA: Yeah.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:05.023
And one aspect that is very
interesting about Kubernetes

00:14:05.023 --> 00:14:08.480
is it brings a lot of
things like log aggregations

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:10.110
that you don't have to do.

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:13.200
There is also the auto
scaling, and node selection.

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:15.870
There is a lot of very
interesting features

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:18.810
that you can take advantage
when you're using a game server.

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:19.560
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:20.940
The other thing I
actually want to make

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:22.080
sure I bring up as
well, which I think

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:24.288
is really important, because
we talked about latency.

00:14:24.288 --> 00:14:25.840
Kubernetes is really
important here,

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:27.840
because sometimes
players show up

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:29.490
in really weird
places in the world.

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:31.897
Sometimes you'll just
have a group of players

00:14:31.897 --> 00:14:33.730
somewhere in the world,
and you're like, OK,

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:36.270
we need to get our
infrastructure somewhere

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:38.590
close to them so that we
can get close latency.

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:40.705
Now, maybe GKE or
another cloud provider

00:14:40.705 --> 00:14:42.580
isn't necessarily going
to help you for that.

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:43.716
Maybe they are.

00:14:43.716 --> 00:14:45.090
That gives you
some more options.

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:47.070
And maybe you just
need to get a co-lo

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:48.240
and stick Kubernetes in it.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:50.340
And now suddenly you can run
your whole infrastructure.

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:52.589
So that kind of flexibility
is really important to us,

00:14:52.589 --> 00:14:54.997
to allow you to run these
platforms in exactly the place

00:14:54.997 --> 00:14:57.330
that you need them so that
your players can get the best

00:14:57.330 --> 00:14:58.290
experience.

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:00.915
MELANIE WARWICK: Were there any
tradeoffs that you had to make?

00:15:00.915 --> 00:15:02.751
MARK MANDEL: Some
really minor ones.

00:15:02.751 --> 00:15:05.126
I'm thinking about some of
the stuff around [? Arback. ?]

00:15:05.126 --> 00:15:07.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:09.506
There's some stuff that
we're still working out.

00:15:09.506 --> 00:15:11.130
This gets really
nitty-gritty, but like

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:14.160
[? Arback ?] is applied at
a pod level, for example.

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:17.820
And we do some side car
stuff that enables access

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:20.760
to see what happens
inside the custom resource

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:23.150
definition and basically
the game server definition.

00:15:23.150 --> 00:15:26.190
But we're looking at ways we can
lock that down a little better.

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:28.950
But otherwise, it's
been pretty OK.

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:31.830
Had to write some special health
checking stuff specifically

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:32.850
for games servers.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.670
So by default, at
a pod level, you

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:37.330
can tell your pod to restart
if something fails or not.

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:41.782
But what we want instead
of-- if a game server fails

00:15:41.782 --> 00:15:43.990
for whatever reason once
players are connected to it,

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:45.490
you don't really
want it to restart.

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:47.790
Because then if players
reconnect to a state

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:50.860
that was zero, then
weird stuff could happen.

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:52.160
So we want it to just die.

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:54.609
But before a player connects,
we want it to restart.

00:15:54.609 --> 00:15:56.400
So we had to do some
custom logic for that,

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:57.480
which was kind of fun.

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:58.639
But yeah.

00:15:58.639 --> 00:16:00.930
Odd stuff that Kubernetes
wasn't necessarily built for.

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:02.750
But mostly it's been
actually really, really good.

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:02.910
CYRIL TOVENA: Yeah.

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:05.640
I think it has been very
good, because Kubernetes has

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:06.930
been built to be extensible.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:07.680
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.096
CYRIL TOVENA: And
it has been very

00:16:09.096 --> 00:16:10.500
easy to actually extend it.

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:12.333
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
the interesting thing

00:16:12.333 --> 00:16:15.820
is I don't think that many
people use stateful containers

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:17.160
on Kubernetes.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:20.830
Did you find any specific
issues where you got into that?

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:22.350
You were mentioning
that it's better

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:24.150
to die than just to restart.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:25.680
Are there any other
things, like how

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:27.900
do you handle the fact
that if a connection goes

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:31.270
down temporarily,
do you just give up?

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:33.180
MARK MANDEL: So on
the Agones side,

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:35.580
we don't handle the
communication side of things.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:38.100
We defer to the actual
game server implementation.

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:38.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, OK.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:39.690
MARK MANDEL: We're pretty
hands-off in that way.

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:42.690
So we're like, you can make UDP
connections or TCB connections.

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:44.790
Most game servers use UDP.

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:47.314
And then it's kind of
application specific, I think,

00:16:47.314 --> 00:16:48.480
is probably pretty accurate.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:49.355
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Mhm.

00:16:49.355 --> 00:16:50.272
MELANIE WARWICK: Nice.

00:16:50.272 --> 00:16:52.021
Cyril, what was it
like working with Mark?

00:16:52.021 --> 00:16:53.256
CYRIL TOVENA: It was great.

00:16:53.256 --> 00:16:54.208
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:54.208 --> 00:16:55.247
CYRIL TOVENA: Nope.

00:16:55.247 --> 00:16:56.580
MARK MANDEL: I was going to say.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:57.170
I'll give you that money later.

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:58.461
CYRIL TOVENA: No, but for real.

00:16:58.461 --> 00:16:59.109
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:59.109 --> 00:17:00.150
