00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:20.747
Francesc, since you're our Go
expert, what are your thoughts?

00:24:20.747 --> 00:24:21.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:23.370
[LAUGHS] There's two answers.

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:26.403
One is the quick answer,
which probably, no.

00:24:26.403 --> 00:24:27.486
MELANIE WARWICK: Probably?

00:24:27.486 --> 00:24:28.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Probably not.

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:30.020
MELANIE WARWICK: You
seemed much more emphatic

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:30.874
about that earlier.

00:24:30.874 --> 00:24:32.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Let's
just say that if you're

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:34.260
starting from
scratch, and you're

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:37.324
learning about machine learning,
probably just stick to Python.

00:24:37.324 --> 00:24:38.990
That is the ecosystem
where you're going

00:24:38.990 --> 00:24:39.820
to find all the libraries.

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:41.160
You're going to
find all the help.

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:42.576
So it totally makes
sense to start

00:24:42.576 --> 00:24:46.640
with Python or any other
language that you know already

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:48.050
and just keep on learning.

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:49.130
I think actual tools.

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:51.920
Don't learn Go just to
do machine learning.

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:53.960
Because it's probably
not the best thing to do.

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:55.679
That said, though,
since I joined Source,

00:24:55.679 --> 00:24:57.470
we've been doing a lot
of machine learning.

00:24:57.470 --> 00:25:00.110
And I've been trying to teach
people that know already Go how

00:25:00.110 --> 00:25:01.497
to do machine learning in Go.

00:25:01.497 --> 00:25:03.080
Because I think
that's a very nice way

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:05.810
to learn really how
it works for real,

00:25:05.810 --> 00:25:07.550
like what are the
maths behind it.

00:25:07.550 --> 00:25:10.220
So I give a talk about
that doing dotGo in Paris

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:13.920
back in November 2017,
which was like my first day

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:15.739
as a non-Googler.

00:25:15.739 --> 00:25:17.780
And also I gave a talk in
Paris at the same time.

00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:19.104
It was a fun day.

00:25:19.104 --> 00:25:21.020
And since then, I've
also been doing like just

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:22.561
for fun episodes
and stuff like this.

00:25:22.561 --> 00:25:25.700
And basically, the experience
is you can definitely do it.

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:26.270
You can try.

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:28.460
You can write all of
the algorithms in Go,

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:30.510
and it kind of works,
but not as fast.

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:32.990
Mostly because when
you're running it in Go,

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:34.940
you're going to end
up not using correctly

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:38.090
the CPU, because there's
like X specific instructions

00:25:38.090 --> 00:25:40.640
from some CPUs done
specifically for this.

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:42.770
And by default, you
will not be using those.

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:46.490
Also, you will not be using
GPU, because Go's and GPUs don't

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:47.670
go well together.

00:25:47.670 --> 00:25:49.640
So there's a couple
libraries that people

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:53.300
that might be wanting to try
this might be interested in.

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:56.360
One is gonum, which
is NumPy but for Go.

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:57.410
It's gonum.

00:25:57.410 --> 00:25:59.216
And the cool thing
about this is that it

00:25:59.216 --> 00:26:00.590
solves one of the
problems, which

00:26:00.590 --> 00:26:04.070
is the fact that when you are
calling libraries, if you're

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:07.220
on a platform that supports
a specific instruction,

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:08.910
that instruction will be used.

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:11.564
So in general, any algorithm
that you write using gonum

00:26:11.564 --> 00:26:12.480
is going to be faster.

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.350
So yeah, like you do [INAUDIBLE]
they're going to say,

00:26:15.350 --> 00:26:16.334
rather than doing--

00:26:16.334 --> 00:26:18.500
oh, I'm going to do a for
loop to these two vectors,

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:21.464
you just do like floats.add
or something to that.

00:26:21.464 --> 00:26:23.380
MELANIE WARWICK: Sometimes
people say tensors.

00:26:23.380 --> 00:26:24.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Tensors, yeah.

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:25.520
So that's the whole idea.

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:28.400
You go towards tensors without
really saying the word tensors,

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:29.870
because you talk about floats.

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:32.360
Because it's just
slices and arrays.

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:34.370
Then there's gorgonia,
which is the one

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:36.800
that people should
be using if they want

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:38.390
to be training things in Go.

00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:39.290
It works really well.

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:43.230
It's not full feature yet,
but it works quite well.

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:45.380
And it uses the GPU,
which means that now you

00:26:45.380 --> 00:26:47.600
can start doing things
that are quite fast.

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:49.850
The last thing, though,
is that most people do not

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:51.145
want to train things in Go.

00:26:51.145 --> 00:26:52.520
What they want to
do is they want

00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:55.130
to have little binary that
uses a trained model that

00:26:55.130 --> 00:26:57.980
has been trained with TensorFlow
or something like that.

00:26:57.980 --> 00:27:00.590
And for that, you can
use TensorFlow for Go.

00:27:00.590 --> 00:27:02.660
Because you can use
perfectly trained models,

00:27:02.660 --> 00:27:06.089
load them, and run them, but
you can not train new ones, yet.

00:27:06.089 --> 00:27:07.130
MELANIE WARWICK: Not yet.

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:07.475
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Hopefully--

00:27:07.475 --> 00:27:07.820
MELANIE WARWICK: One day.

00:27:07.820 --> 00:27:09.486
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --it
will come one day,

00:27:09.486 --> 00:27:11.570
but for now, I've seen
a couple pull requests.

00:27:11.570 --> 00:27:13.460
I think the support
for variables,

00:27:13.460 --> 00:27:14.960
right now there's
only placeholders.

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:17.840
So then you can not do much
with variables in TensorFlow,

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:18.740
unfortunately.

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:21.015
So yeah, that will
come sometime soon.

00:27:21.015 --> 00:27:23.140
I don't work at Google, so
